# χ notation for full-paper analysis

Before inventing or changing symbols for central tendency, variability, or sample-size adequacy, read:

[`statistical_analysis/full_paper/analysis/NOTATION.md`](statistical_analysis/full_paper/analysis/NOTATION.md)

## Counts (required)

| Symbol | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| \(N_x\) | number of nodes |
| \(N_s\) | number of seeds |
| \(i\) | node index |
| \(j\) | seed index |

**Never** use bare \(n\)/\(m\) or \(N\)/\(S\) for node/seed counts in math, markdown, or plot labels.

Keep code identifiers as-is: `N_NODES`, `N_SEEDS`, `n_nodes`, `n_seeds`, and historical CSV names (`S_req_*`, `adequate_mean_S100`, …).

## Common pitfall

- \(n_{\mathrm{eff}}\) is CosWM effective sample size — leave it; it is not \(N_x\).
- Adequacy overall estimators are \(\hat\mu\), \(\hat\sigma^2\) (not \(\hat\mu_S\), \(\hat\sigma^2_S\)).
- Required-seed formulas use \(N_{s,\mu}(\varepsilon)\), \(N_{s,\sigma^2}(\varepsilon)\).
